{"id": "sevt_01GUVViCzigZDnkTizKen1Ym", "content": [{"text": "Checkout p99 jumped at 14:19, about a minute before the page. It went from roughly 60\u201380 ms to a flat ~5,000 ms, which is the Stripe client's 5s timeout. The cause is Stripe API latency, not a deploy.\n\n- **Deploys:** none lines up. Checkout deployed at 12:40 (backoff on the payment client), inventory at 13:33 and cart at 14:47. The cart deploy is after the spike. I read the inventory diff (9f66c1c). It only adds an unused `validate_sku` function, so it can't explain a 14:19 step.\n- **DB pool:** checkout utilization went from about 0.12 to 0.72 and held there from 14:24. Cart, auth and inventory stayed flat at about 0.1\u20130.2, so the database isn't the source. The pool filling is most likely requests piling up while they wait on Stripe.\n- **Log:** `stripe-api` calls from checkout were already slow at 14:19:34 (3.5s, then 2.8s). The first `stripe-api timeout after 5000ms` is at 14:19:46. The ERROR lines I saw are checkout 502s with `UpstreamTimeout` at about 5,000 ms, for example on `/api/checkout/summary` and `/api/checkout/validate`.\n- **Not checked:** I did not look at Stripe's status page, so I can't say why Stripe slowed down.\n- **Possible contributor:** the 12:40 backoff change (0885a56) is a candidate for making retries amplify the slowdown. I did not read its diff, so that is unconfirmed.\n- **Confidence:** high that the delay is on the Stripe side. Medium on the details.\n- **Next steps:** check Stripe's status page. Consider a shorter timeout or a circuit breaker on the payment client to keep the pool from saturating.\n\n**Root cause:** `stripe-api` \u2014 upstream Stripe calls began timing out at the 5000ms client limit at 14:19, so checkout requests hung for ~5s and returned 502s while the DB pool filled to 72%.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:43:45.531324Z", "type": "agent.message"}
